  "conclusions": [
    "Removing the contiguous-packet constraint exposes a real but idealized individual-neuron signal: a 10% per-expert relative L2 target retains 51.75% of neurons, while a fixed 50% budget produces 11.81% mean and 17.53% p95 error.",
    "The useful neuron identities are not stable enough for simple fixed residency groups. At the 10% error target, co-selection-signature packing expands 50.31% individual coverage to 79.15%, 81.52%, or 84.51% residency for 8-, 16-, or 32-neuron groups.",
    "Even when restricted to held-out observations whose layer/expert pair appeared in calibration, the resident fractions remain 72.22%, 75.16%, and 79.12%; missing calibration is not the entire failure.",
    "The p95 held-out expert touches every fixed group at all three group sizes.",
    "The free-neuron result is a model-co-design target, not a deployable runtime plan: structured training must make important neurons stable and group-aligned before consumer-native grouped storage can realize the theoretical capacity reduction."
